The letter that prompted Heidegger’s invitation to come back to the Hütte at Todtnauberg is Jean Beaufret’s post-first-visit protest of summer-autumn 1945, usually dated late 1945 / 1 Dec 1945 / Feb 1946, the opening piece of the 1945-1975 Beaufret–Heidegger correspondence.

Beaufret, 1907-1982, philologist-philosopher and with Kojève the first systematic French introducer/translator of Heidegger after the war, had written Heidegger in 1943-44/45 asking for an exposition of *Sein und Zeit* and a clarification of the Rectorship and came to Freiburg in summer 1945 for the first Todtnauberg encounter.

The wounded-intimacy letter written on leaving that first encounter is the one quoted:
* “I believed that a man who can write *Being and Time* could understand something about men”; 
* “I could not believe that metaphysics enclosed a spirit within itself, confusing intimacy with hermeticism. On the contrary, intimacy offers a more delicate possibility of engaging with others without losing oneself in them”;
* leaving “as sad and tormented as I arrived”, not leaving angry but wounded, “the fault lies within us, for we believed too quickly in a reality that did not exist”;
* “ultimate solitude is the only way to be close to others”;
* “something has passed between the two of us that will never be erased from my being. Allow me to dispel any possible misunderstanding regarding this letter by calling you a friend. You will remain a friend to me in the secret solitude of my existence”;
* closing with the formula: “the worth of an existence is not measured by the height of its achievements, but by the depth of its roots”.

In French the text is the classic Beaufret passage:

*J’ai cru qu’un homme capable d’écrire Être et Temps pouvait comprendre quelque chose aux hommes… Je ne pouvais pas croire que la métaphysique enferme un esprit en elle-même, confondant intimité et hermétisme… La solitude ultime est la seule manière d’être proche des autres… La valeur d’une existence ne se mesure pas à la hauteur de ses réalisations, mais à la profondeur de ses racines.*

It is to that letter Heidegger replied warmly and invited Beaufret to return to the Black Forest. The invitation inaugurates the October 1945 and 1946 Todtnauberg stays and the conversations on which *Dialogue avec Heidegger / Entretien sur Heidegger* is based.

The English rendering of the passage circulates widely and is often mis-printed under Emmanuel Levinas’s 1946-47 break with Heidegger. Levinas does use in the same years the same motifs of disappointment about the author of *Being and Time*, intimacy vs hermeticism and solitude as proximity, which explains the persistent conflation. Historically the documented home invitation belongs to Beaufret, not to Levinas, and not to Ortega y Gasset whose contacts with Heidegger are late 1920s/early 1930s.

